"Disaggregates an organization's strategically relevant activities to examine sources of cost advantage and differentiation.\n\nUse when: Explain how activity choices support a competitive position. Identify where partner arrangements or activity linkages create value.\n\nAvoid when: A case's exact activity order or shop-floor waiting time is required.\n\nVisual grammar: Primary activity regions represent value-delivery activities. Support activity bands span the primary activities they enable. Annotations identify distinctive choices, linkages and cost or value drivers.\n\nBuild: Define the business boundary and value proposition. Identify primary and supporting activities at a consistent level. Analyze how selected activities and their linkages support advantage.\n\nCheck: Activities reflect the actual business rather than a forced template. Cost and differentiation claims connect to specific choices. The wider supplier-customer value system is distinguished from the firm.\n\nPitfalls: Using departments as a substitute for economically meaningful activities. Confusing operational efficiency alone with a distinct strategy.\n\nExample: A specialty retailer examines sourcing, merchandising, store operations and aftercare, showing how expert advice supports premium pricing.\n\nAlternatives: Porter Five Forces, Value-stream map."
